### 10.2 Unit tests

Intent classification in all six languages; identity resolution and normalization; hash recomputation; evidence scanners; timestamp filtering and boundaries; policy lookup and treatment evaluation; retention, tombstone, and legal-hold checks; recreation-path detection; adjudication; remediation ordering and dependencies; serializer byte-equality with sample rows; evidence-reference validation.

### 10.3 Integration and edge tests

Test the full pipeline from request to report. Include:

- Missing input files, empty datasets, malformed CSV and JSON, unsupported formats, corrupted SQL, unreadable PDFs, and OCR failures.
- AI timeouts and invalid model output.
- Database errors, repeated requests, partial scans, and export failures.
- Non-deletion intents in all six languages, negation, and withdrawal.
- Multiple historical emails, duplicate users, shared identifiers, and users with similar names.
- Evidence captured exactly at, just before, and just after `audit_at`; missing timestamps.
- Active and inactive connectors, connectors unrelated to the subject, and unrelated backup restores.
- Conflicting rules, missing rules, unknown categories.
- Retained records and tombstones that contain prohibited personal information.
- Legal holds.
- Cyclic identity graphs and malformed hashes.
- Duplicate findings and contradictory remediation actions.
- Prompt injection in scanned documents.
- Determinism: two runs produce identical output hashes.

### 10.4 API tests

Authentication bypass, role enforcement on every route, IDOR, pagination bounds, input validation, error envelope, idempotent submission, rate limiting, CSRF, oversized bodies, and the no-remediation-endpoint check.

### 10.5 Frontend and browser tests

- Component and hook tests for tables, filters, status badges, and graph fallbacks.
- Playwright end-to-end tests against the real API and a seeded database: login, start an audit, open a case, inspect evidence, view the plan, export a report, role restrictions, keyboard navigation, mobile viewport.
- Automated accessibility checks with axe on every route, with zero critical violations.
- Assert that no console errors occur during the main flows.
- Inspect the running application in the browser, capture screenshots of the main screens, and fix visible layout or functional defects.

### 10.6 Security tests

Path traversal through the manifest; malicious `.sql` using `ATTACH` or `load_extension`; oversized files; XSS payloads in request text and evidence excerpts; prompt-injection fixtures; log-redaction assertions; dataset-unchanged assertions; secret-leak scans; resource-exhaustion limits.

### 10.7 Evaluation metrics

Calculate and record, from real runs only: overall status accuracy; per-class precision, recall, and F1; confusion matrix; false-`COMPLETE` count and rate; treatment-rule accuracy; recreation-path detection performance; step-level, field-level, and sequence accuracy (use the official definitions if given); evidence-reference validity; step-ordering compliance; schema compliance; processing time, peak memory, and failure rate.

Prioritize avoiding unsupported `COMPLETE` results without ignoring the official scoring rubric.

### 10.8 Required testing artifacts

- `code/tests/` with unit, integration, edge, security, and API suites.
- `scripts/run_tests` (Python or shell) that runs everything.
